{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 41, :comments ["<b>Then Moses set apart three cities.</b> Ibn Ezra explained (commentary on Deuteronomy 4:41) that on the day Moses set apart three cities, he then spoke the words of the covenant. This is not correct. Rather, it is according to its plain sense, for Moses assembled all Israel to explain the Torah to them, and he opened for them with words of rebuke. And when he had spoken his rebukes before them concerning what they had done and the warnings against idolatry and concerning the unity of the Name, and he had completed for them that they should keep His statutes and commandments in order that it go well with them — then, in their assembly and before them, he said: now let us fulfill the commandment that HaShem commanded us, and they shall be Bezer in the wilderness, and Ramoth in Gilead, and Golan in Bashan, cities of refuge, to flee there every unintentional killer. And even though they do not provide refuge until those in the land of Canaan are set apart, he said: a commandment that has come into our hands, let us fulfill it, in order that it go well with us. And after that, he called out in a great voice to all Israel who were there and said to them (Deuteronomy 5:1): Hear, O Israel, the statutes and the judgments, etc. — for now he would begin with the commandments and the explanation of the Torah, as I explained at the beginning of the parashah of Eleh ha-Devarim."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {0 "Ramban refers to Ibn Ezra as ר\"א, resolved by the explicit citation in parentheses. The verse references appear in the source within parentheses and are rendered as given."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:45:26.477683352Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}